---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RecordLookupTool from '../components/RecordLookupTool.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="MX Record Lookup" description="MX record lookup: see which mail servers accept email for any domain, with priorities and TTLs.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">MX Record Lookup</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">MX Record Lookup</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-dim">MX records route inbound mail. Check yours to verify mail routing before and after provider changes.</p>
    <div class="mt-8"><RecordLookupTool type="MX" label="MX Record Lookup" hint="Lookups run in your browser via public DNS-over-HTTPS. Nothing is logged." /></div>
    <article class="rich-text mt-12 max-w-none">
      <h2>How to read the results</h2>
      <p>Each MX answer has two parts: a priority number and a mail hostname. Lower priorities are tried first; equal priorities share load. The hostname must itself resolve for delivery to work.</p>
      <p>No MX records with a NOERROR status means senders fall back to the domain's A record. For mail-only subdomains that can be intentional; on a main domain it usually means email is not set up.</p>
      <div class="grid gap-2"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-4 py-3 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="cursor-pointer list-none font-medium text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can MX point to an IP?<span class="float-right text-accent">+</span></summary><p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No: MX values are hostnames that must resolve to addresses themselves.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-4 py-3 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="cursor-pointer list-none font-medium text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What MX priority should I use?<span class="float-right text-accent">+</span></summary><p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Distinct numbers, 10 and 20 by convention. Lower wins; equal values share load.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-4 py-3 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="cursor-pointer list-none font-medium text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why does mail arrive at the old server?<span class="float-right text-accent">+</span></summary><p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Cached MX records: lower TTL before provider changes and watch the propagation checker.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-4 py-3 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="cursor-pointer list-none font-medium text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do MX records affect outgoing mail?<span class="float-right text-accent">+</span></summary><p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No: MX routes incoming mail only. Outgoing authentication is SPF, DKIM, and DMARC.</p></details></div>
      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/mx-record/">MX records explained (guide)</a></li>
        <li><a href="/dns-record-lookup/">All-in-one record lookup</a> and <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">propagation checker</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
